['Here is an example of how you might implement these functions using ASP.NET Core and Entity Framework Core:

```csharp
public class UserProfilesController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly YourDbContext _context;

    public UserProfilesController(YourDbContext context)
    {
        _context = context;
    }

    [HttpPut("{id}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> UpdateProfile(int id, ProfileUpdateDto updateData)
    {
        var profileToUpdate = await _context.UserProfiles.FindAsync(id);

        if (profileToUpdate == null)
            return NotFound();

        // Patch the user's profile with the data from the request body
        JsonPatchDocument<Profile> patchDoc = new JsonPatchDocument<Profile>();
        patchDoc.Replace(p => p.FirstName, updateData.FirstName);
        patchDoc.Replace(p => p.LastName, updateData.LastName);

        // Apply the patch to the profile
        patchDoc.ApplyTo(profileToUpdate);

        try
        {
            await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
        }
        catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException) when (!ProfileExists(id))
        {
            return NotFound();
        }

        return NoContent();
    }

    [HttpPatch("{id}/deactivate")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> DeactivateProfile(int id)
    {
        var profileToDeactivate = await _context.UserProfiles.FindAsync(id);

        if (profileToDeactivate == null)
            return NotFound();

        // Set the deactivated flag to true
        profileToDeactivate.IsDeactivated = true;

        try
        {
            await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
        }
        catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException) when (!ProfileExists(id))
        {
            return NotFound();
        }

        // Return the updated profile with the new status
        return Ok(profileToDeactivate);
    }

    private bool ProfileExists(int id) => _context.UserProfiles.Any(e => e.Id == id);
}
```
The `ProfileUpdateDto` class would be a data transfer object that only includes the fields which can be updated. This DTO should include fields for each updatable property, and you'll use this to deserialize incoming JSON into an instance of this type when accepting PUT requests. 

Please replace "YourDbContext", "ProfileUpdateDto" with your actual DbContext name and Profile Update Dto respectively. This example uses Microsoft.AspNetCore.JsonPatch for patching the profile, but you can also use other libraries or custom logic to handle partial updates. The concurrency handling is performed using optimistic concurrency control by catching a `DbUpdateConcurrencyException` that's thrown when SaveChangesAsync is called and checking if the profile exists before returning NotFound. 

Remember to add using statements for Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc, Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore, System.Threading.Tasks. 

These functions are asynchronous to ensure they can handle high-load scenarios without blocking threads. The `[HttpPut("{id}")]` and `[HttpPatch("{id}/deactivate")]` attributes specify the HTTP methods that these actions should respond to, with "{id}" in the URL template representing a user ID parameter.
']

['Here's a basic implementation in C# using `System.Xml` namespace for handling XML data and `System.Xml.XPath` namespace for XPath queries:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.XPath;

public class XMLParser {

    public List<IXmlNamespaceResolver> NamespaceResolvers = new List<IXmlNamespaceResolver>();

    // Function to parse XML using XPath, handle namespaces and validate the XML structure
    public List<XmlNode> ParseXML(string xmlString, string xpathExpression) {

        var results = new List<XmlNode>();

        try{
            var settings = new XmlReaderSettings(){ 
                DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Parse, // validate XML structure by parsing DTDs
                ValidationType = ValidationType.DTD // only DTD validation is performed
            };

            using (var reader = XmlReader.Create(new System.IO.StringReader(xmlString), settings)) {

                var document = new XPathDocument(reader);

                foreach(var resolver in NamespaceResolvers) {
                    var navigator = document.CreateNavigator();

                    // Iterate over all nodes and register namespaces in the current context
                    while (navigator.MoveToFollowing(XPathNodeType.Element)) {
                        RegisterNamespaces(navigator, resolver);
                    }

                    var iterator = navigator.Select(xpathExpression);

                    while (iterator.MoveNext()) {
                        results.Add(iterator.Current.UnderlyingObject as XmlNode);
                    }
                }
            }
        }catch(XmlException ex){
            // Log warning for malformed XML
            Console.WriteLine("Warning: " + ex.Message);
        }

        return results;
    }

    private void RegisterNamespaces(XPathNavigator navigator, IXmlNamespaceResolver resolver) {
        foreach (var namespaceItem in resolver.GetNamespacesInScope(XmlNamespaceScope.All)) {
            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(namespaceItem.Key)){
                navigator.MoveToAttribute(namespaceItem.Key, string.Empty);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this code:
- A `XMLParser` class is defined with a method `ParseXML` that takes an XML document as a string and an XPath expression as parameters. It uses the `XPathDocument` class to parse the XML data. 
- The `DtdProcessing` property of `XmlReaderSettings` is set to `DtdProcessing.Parse`, so the parser will validate the XML structure by parsing DTDs. 
- If there are any validation errors (malformed XML), they are caught and logged as warnings in a try-catch block. 
- The function also handles namespaces by iterating over all nodes and registering them with a namespace resolver using `RegisterNamespaces` method. This allows the XPath expression to correctly identify elements within these namespaces. 
- It uses an efficient way of parsing XML data - only DTD processing is performed, thus reducing memory usage and improving processing time when dealing with large XML documents.
']

['Here's how you can do it:
```java
import java.security.*;
import javax.xml.bind.*; // Required for DatatypeConverter to convert byte[] to hex
import java.util.*;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.*;
import java.nio.charset.*;
import java.math.*;
import java.lang.String;
import java.lang.Exception;

class InvalidInputLengthException extends Exception {
    public InvalidInputLengthException(String errorMessage) {
        super(errorMessage);
    }
}
public class MD5HashGenerator {
    private static final Logger logger = LogManager.getLogger(MD5HashGenerator.class);
    private MessageDigest md;

    public MD5HashGenerator() throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        this.md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
    }

    // Concurrent modification to the map is handled by synchronized Collections API
    public Map<String, String> generateHashes(List<String> strings) {
        Map<String, String> hashesMap = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

        for (String s : strings) {
            try {
                if (s.length() > 256) {
                    throw new InvalidInputLengthException("Input string exceeds 256 characters in length.");
                }

                byte[] hashBytes = md.digest(s.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
                String hexHash = DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(hashBytes);

                hashesMap.put(s, hexHash);
            } catch (InvalidInputLengthException e) {
                logger.error("Exception caught: ", e);
            } 
        }

        return hashesMap;
    }
}
```
This solution uses Java's built-in concurrency support with synchronized collections and methods to handle multiple threads modifying the map without data corruption. In case of an invalid input string length, it throws a custom exception `InvalidInputLengthException` which is handled and logged at the ERROR level using Log4j2 logging framework.

The hash generation is done by using Java's built-in MessageDigest class for MD5 hashing and DatatypeConverter from JAXB API to convert byte[] to hex string. The exception handling is efficient as it only logs exceptions without stopping the process, which makes it an improvement over try-catch in a loop that stops processing after first error.
']

