['Here is a Java class implementing this functionality:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.Date;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class StateLoader {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = Logger.getLogger(StateLoader.class.getName());
    private Date lastModifiedTime;

    public MoSTState loadStateFromFile(String filePath) {
        File stateFile = new File(filePath);

        // Check if the file was modified since the last load attempt
        Date currentLastModifiedTime = new Date(stateFile.lastModified());
        if (currentLastModifiedTime.equals(lastModifiedTime)) {
            LOGGER.info("The state file has not been modified, no need to reload.");
            return null;
        }

        // Load the state object from the file
        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(stateFile))) {
            Object loadedObject = in.readObject();

            if (loadedObject instanceof MoSTState) {
                lastModifiedTime = currentLastModifiedTime;  // Update cached timestamp
                LOGGER.info("Successfully loaded state from file: " + filePath);
                return (MoSTState) loadedObject;
            } else {
                LOGGER.warning("The loaded object is not an instance of MoSTState, discarding it.");
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            LOGGER.log(Level.SEVERE, "An IOException occurred while loading the state file:", e);
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            LOGGER.log(Level.SEVERE, "A ClassNotFoundException occurred during deserialization:", e);
        }

        return null;  // Return null if an error occurred or if loaded object is not of type MoSTState
    }
}
```
This method loads a state object from the specified file path using `ObjectInputStream`. If the file has been modified since the last successful load, it will be reloaded and its modification time will be updated in memory. The method logs detailed information about any loading attempts and failures, handles potential exceptions gracefully, and manages resources properly to avoid leaks.
']

