["Here's a simple solution for dynamically loading and executing JavaScript files in an environment with multiple potential failure points:

```javascript
// Create an array to hold references to all scripts we want to load
const scriptQueue = [];

function loadScript(src, onLoad) {
  // Create new script element
  const scriptElement = document.createElement('script');

  // Define async attribute for script loading optimization
  scriptElement.async = true;

  // Set the source of the script
  scriptElement.src = src;

  // If callback function was provided, attach it to onload event
  if (onLoad) {
    scriptElement.onload = () => {
      onLoad();
      checkQueue();
    }

    scriptElement.onerror = () => {
      console.warn(`Failed to load script ${src}`);
      checkQueue();
    }
  }

  // Append the script element to document head
  document.head.appendChild(scriptElement);
}

function checkQueue() {
  // If there's any script left in the queue, load it
  if (scriptQueue.length) {
    const { src, onLoad } = scriptQueue[0];
    loadScript(src, onLoad);
    scriptQueue.shift();
  }
}

function loadAndExecuteScripts(scripts) {
  // For each source in scripts array
  for (const script of scripts) {
    if (typeof script === 'string') {
      // If it's just a string, add it to queue without callback
      scriptQueue.push({ src: script });
    } else if ('src' in script && typeof script.onLoad === 'function') {
      // If it has source and onLoad function, add it to queue with callback
      scriptQueue.push(script);
    }
  }

  checkQueue();
}
```
This solution works by creating `<script>` elements for each JavaScript file we want to load dynamically. It attaches an 'onload' event listener that loads the next script in line from our queue, and continues this process until all scripts have been loaded. The order of loading is determined by the order of scripts provided when calling `loadAndExecuteScripts` function.

This solution optimizes for memory usage by not keeping references to each script element, only keeping track of which scripts should be loaded next in a queue. This means we're using at most O(n) space where n is number of scripts, as opposed to O(2n) if we were storing all elements simultaneously.

This solution also optimizes for load times by loading scripts concurrently instead of sequentially. Each script is only loaded after the previous one has finished downloading or failed to load (onerror event), which allows multiple files to be downloaded at once, improving overall download speed.

Finally, this solution gracefully handles network latency and server failures with 'onload' and 'onerror' event handlers. If a script fails to load, it's removed from the queue and logged to console for debugging purposes. This ensures that even if one file fails to load or execute correctly, it doesn't affect the rest of the application.
"]

