    public static function get($key = null) {
        // If key is not provided return all config settings
        if (is_null($key)) {
            return self::$config;
        }

        // Return specific setting value by key
        if (!array_key_exists($key, self::$config)) {
            throw new Exception("Configuration option $key does not exist.");
        }

        return self::$config[$key];
    }
}

// Initialize web application's environment
if (session_status() == PHP_SESSION_NONE) {
    session_start();
}

// Register a custom autoloader
spl_autoload_register(function ($className) {
    $file = 'path_to_classes/' . $className . '.php';

    if (file_exists($file)) require_once $file;
});

// Configure the application using hierarchical configuration system
Configuration::load();

// Implement caching mechanism for config loading
if (!isset($_SESSION['configCache']) || !is_array($_SESSION['configCache'])) {
    $_SESSION['configCache'] = Configuration::get();
} else {
    // If the configuration has changed, update the cache and reset it.
    if (serialize($_SESSION['configCache']) != serialize(Configuration::get())) {
        $_SESSION['configCache'] = Configuration::get();
        opcache_reset();
    } else {
        // If not, use the cached configuration.
        Configuration::loadFromArray($_SESSION['configCache']);
    }
}
```
']

['Rust provides the `reqwest` crate which is a powerful HTTP client library but lacks certain features such as disabling certificate validation, setting custom headers, and handling redirects up to a specified limit out of the box. To overcome this limitation, we need to use lower-level APIs from Rust's standard libraries (such as `hyper` and `tokio`).

Here is an implementation using `reqwest` crate:

```rust
use reqwest::Url;
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::time::Duration;
use futures_util::StreamExt;
use tokio::net::TcpStream;
use anyhow::Result;
use tracing::info;
use hyper::client::HttpConnector;
use reqwest::header::{HeaderValue, CONTENT_LENGTH};

pub struct HttpClient {
    disable_certificate_validation: bool,
    headers: HashMap<String, String>,
}

impl HttpClient {
    pub fn new(disable_certificate_validation: bool) -> Self {
        Self {
            disable_certificate_validation,
            headers: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    pub fn add_header(&mut self, name: String, value: String) {
        self.headers.insert(name, value);
    }
}

impl HttpClient {
    pub async fn get<T>(&self, url: &str) -> Result<Option<T>> 
    where
        T: serde::de::DeserializeOwned,
    {
        let client = reqwest::ClientBuilder::new()
            .timeout(Duration::from_secs(10))
            .connector(HttpConnector::new(tokio::net::lookup("localhost", "80").and_then(move |addr| {
                tokio::net::TcpStream::connect(&*addr, Default::default())
                    .map_err(|e| std::io::Error::new(std::io::ErrorKind::Other, e))
            })))
            .build()?;

        let request = client.get(url);

        if self.disable_certificate_validation {
            let client = reqwest::ClientBuilder::new().danger_accept_invalid_certs(true).build()?;
            request.send().await?.json().await
        } else {
            let mut headers = request.headers();

            for (name, value) in &self.headers {
                headers.append(name, HeaderValue::from_str(&value)?);
            }

            request.send().await?.json::<T>().await
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, the `HttpClient` structure manages a list of headers and whether certificate validation is disabled or not. When we send a GET request, the client will use these settings accordingly. If we want to disable certificate validation, we set the field `disable_certificate_validation` to true. Headers are added via the `add_header` function.

Please note that this code does not support handling redirects up to a specified limit and does not have streaming capability for large response bodies yet. Adding these features would require more complex design, and it might be better suited for a higher-level library or framework which already supports these features (such as `hyper`).
']

