['Sure, here's an example code block that meets your requirements using C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <errno.h>

#define COMMAND_TIMEOUT 30 // timeout in seconds
#define LOGFILE "/path/to/logfile"

pid_t command_pid = -1;
int stop = 0;
FILE *log_fp;

void signal_handler(int signum) {
    if (signum == SIGINT || signum == SIGTERM) {
        stop = 1; // set a global flag to stop the child process
    } else if (command_pid > 0 && kill(command_pid, 0) == 0) {
        kill(command_pid, signum); // forward signals to the child process
    }
}

void limit_resources() {
    struct rlimit limit;
    getrlimit(RLIMIT_CPU, &limit);
    limit.rlim_cur = 20; // CPU limit 20 seconds for example
    setrlimit(RLIMIT_CPU, &limit);
}

int run_command(char *cmd) {
    if ((command_pid = fork()) < 0) {
        return 0;
    } else if (command_pid == 0) { // child process
        limit_resources();

        FILE *fp = fopen(LOGFILE, "w+");
        dup2(fileno(fp), fileno(stdout));
        dup2(fileno(fp), fileno(stderr));

        setpgrp(); // create a new process group for the child and parent process

        execl("/bin/sh", "sh", "-c", cmd, (char *) NULL);
    } else { // parent process
        signal(SIGINT, signal_handler);
        signal(SIGTERM, signal_handler);

        time_t start = time(NULL);
        while (1) {
            int status;
            if (waitpid(command_pid, &status, WNOHANG) == command_pid || stop == 1) {
                break;
            } else if ((time(NULL) - start) > COMMAND_TIMEOUT) {
                kill(command_pid, SIGTERM); // terminate child process after timeout
                return 0;
            } else {
                sleep(1);
            }
        }

        if (WIFEXITED(status)) {
            log_fp = fopen(LOGFILE, "a");
            fprintf(log_fp, "Command exited with status %d\n", WEXITSTATUS(status));
            fclose(log_fp);
        } else if (WIFSIGNALED(status)) {
            log_fp = fopen(LOGFILE, "a");
            fprintf(log_fp, "Command was terminated by signal %d\n", WTERMSIG(status));
            fclose(log_fp);
        }
    }

    return 1;
}
```

Note: You need to add the `struct passwd` and `struct group` libraries to use `setgid()` and `setuid()`. Also, please replace `"/path/to/logfile"` with your actual log file path. This example does not support environment variables or command-line arguments for now. To fully implement this function, you will need to add these functionalities by yourself.
']

['Here is a possible implementation of your requirements using C#, .NET Framework and asynchronous programming for better performance. This code assumes that you have appropriate permissions to interact with the web server, `web.config` file and network resources. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Polly;

public class WebServerIntegration
{
    private readonly string _templateServerUrl = "http://your_server/web.config";
    private readonly int _retryAttempts = 3;
    private readonly double[] _backoffIntervalsSeconds = { 1, 2, 4 }; // Exponential backoff intervals
    private static readonly SemaphoreSlim _semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(1, 1); // One thread at a time semaphore
    private static readonly Logger _logger = new Logger(); // Your logger implementation

    public async Task<string> IntegrateNewSiteAsync(string siteName, string siteDirectoryPath)
    {
        if (!File.Exists(Path.Combine(siteDirectoryPath, "web.config")))
        {
            await _semaphore.WaitAsync(); // Lock to prevent concurrent access

            try
            {
                var policy = Policy
                    .Handle<WebException>()
                    .Or<IOException>()
                    .WaitAndRetryAsync(_retryAttempts, retryCount => TimeSpan.FromSeconds(_backoffIntervalsSeconds[retryCount]));

                await policy.Execute(async () =>
                {
                    using (var client = new WebClient())
                    {
                        await client.DownloadFileTaskAsync(new Uri(_templateServerUrl), Path.Combine(siteDirectoryPath, "web.config"));
                    }
                });
            }
            finally
            {
                _semaphore.Release(); // Unlock after downloading file or if an exception occurs
            }
        }

        // Rest of the code for adding website to web server and returning appropriate response
        throw new NotImplementedException(); 
    }
}
```
In this example, `Polly` library is used for retries with exponential backoff. The function uses a semaphore to ensure only one thread at a time can add a site to the web server to avoid conflicts in the configuration. You'll need to install and import the Polly library for the policy functionality.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all your requirements, such as logging or error handling. In production code you should have more comprehensive error handling and logging with configurable log levels. This code also assumes you have appropriate permissions on the web server to make changes and access network resources. 

You may need additional dependencies and permissions depending on your specific environment and use case. Please adapt it as needed for your requirements.
