['The following is a basic implementation that reads from a stream line by line, computes MD5 hash and writes it into a logger. You would need to fill in the missing parts based on how you will be reading the input stream (for instance, whether from a file or socket) and what logging framework you are using.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading;

public class StreamProcessor
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public StreamProcessor(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(logger));
    }

    public void ProcessStream(Stream stream)
    {
        if (stream == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(stream));

        using var reader = new StreamReader(stream);
        string line;
        while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)
        {
            ThreadPool.QueueUserWorkItem(_ => 
                _logger.LogInformation($"MD5 hash: {ComputeMd5Hash(line)}"));
        }
    }

    private string ComputeMd5Hash(string input)
    {
        using var md5 = MD5.Create();

        byte[] inputBytes = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(input);
        byte[] hashBytes = md5.ComputeHash(inputBytes);

        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        foreach (var b in hashBytes)
            sb.Append(b.ToString("X2"));

        return sb.ToString();
    }
}
```
This solution is simple and efficient, but might not be suitable for high-throughput scenarios due to the use of `ThreadPool` instead of custom thread management. Furthermore, there's no error handling for potential read/write exceptions. These can be added based on the specific requirements.

For more complex real-time processing tasks, a better approach would be using reactive extensions (Rx) which provides an API for creating and manipulating observable sequences of data. However, this is beyond the scope of the original problem and would require extensive testing and additional design work to ensure it meets all requirements, especially when dealing with high throughput or real-time constraints.
