['Here is the C# code that meets your requirements:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public class AESDecryptor {
    private static readonly Dictionary<PaddingMode, int> padMap = new Dictionary<PaddingMode, int>() { 
        { PaddingMode.PKCS7, 1 }, 
        { PaddingMode.None, 0 } 
    };

    public static string Decrypt(string cipherTextBase64, byte[] key, byte[] iv, PaddingMode padding) {
        try {
            // Convert base64 to bytes
            byte[] cipherBytes = Convert.FromBase64String(cipherTextBase64);

            // Check if the length of the input is a multiple of block size (16 for AES)
            if ((cipherBytes.Length % 16) != 0) {
                return "Ciphertext length does not align with the expected block size of AES";
            }

            // Check key and IV length
            if (key == null || key.Length <= 0) {
                return "Invalid or missing encryption key";
            } else if (iv == null || iv.Length <= 0) {
                return "Invalid or missing initialization vector";
            } 

            // Check padding mode
            if (!padMap.ContainsKey(padding)) {
                return $"Invalid or missing padding mode: {padding}";
            }

            using (Aes aes = Aes.Create()) {
                aes.BlockSize = 128; 
                aes.Padding = padding;

                ICryptoTransform decryptor = aes.CreateDecryptor(key, iv);
                byte[] plainBytes = decryptor.TransformFinalBlock(cipherBytes, 0, cipherBytes.Length);

                // Return the decrypted plaintext as string
                return Encoding.UTF8.GetString(plainBytes, 0, plainBytes.Length - padMap[padding]);
            }
        } catch (CryptographicException e) {
            return $"Error in decryption: {e.Message}";
        } catch (FormatException e) {
            return $"Invalid base64 format: {e.Message}";
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return $"Unexpected error: {e.Message}";
        }
    }
}
```
This function decrypts the given ciphertext using AES-CBC with the provided encryption key, initialization vector and padding mode. It handles invalid inputs by checking the lengths of key, IV, and the ciphertext length to be a multiple of block size (16 for AES), as well as checks if the padding mode is valid or not. If any check fails, it returns an appropriate error message. Otherwise, it decrypts the ciphertext using the provided parameters and returns the plaintext as a string.
']

['Here's a basic example of how you might accomplish this task in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;

public class CustomDataStructure
{
    // Your complex object graph goes here.
}

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        const string filePath = "path_to_your_file";
        var deserializedData = DeserializeFile<CustomDataStructure>(filePath);

        Console.WriteLine("Deserialization completed.");
        Console.ReadKey();
    }

    static List<T> DeserializeFile<T>(string filePath) where T : class
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(filePath)) throw new ArgumentException("Value cannot be null or whitespace.", nameof(filePath));

        var formatter = new BinaryFormatter();
        var list = new List<T>();
        const int bufferSize = 1024 * 1024; // Adjust this value according to your specific needs.
        byte[] buffer = new byte[bufferSize];

        using (var fileStream = File.Open(filePath, FileMode.Open))
        {
            int bytesRead;
            while ((bytesRead = fileStream.Read(buffer, 0, bufferSize)) > 0)
            {
                for (int i = 0; i < bytesRead; i++)
                {
                    using (var memoryStream = new MemoryStream(buffer, i, bytesRead - i))
                    {
                        var item = formatter.Deserialize(memoryStream);

                        if (!(item is T)) continue;

                        list.Add((T)item);
                        Console.WriteLine("Item deserialized."); // Logging the progress of deserialization
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return list;
    }
}
```

This code reads a binary file in chunks, each chunk being `bufferSize` bytes long (1MB in this case). For every chunk it opens a new MemoryStream and deserializes the item. If the item is of type T, it's added to the list. The memory usage is limited by reading the file in smaller chunks which helps handle larger files without causing out-of-memory errors.

This code snippet uses BinaryFormatter for serialization and deserialization. It might not be efficient or reliable for large files, depending on your exact requirements. If you need to process extremely large files, consider using a different format that supports streaming (like JSON.NET). You can also use third-party libraries like MessagePack-CSharp which offer faster and smaller output than BinaryFormatter but require more effort to implement.
