            free(buffer);
            fclose(input);
            return;
        }

        // Write the resized frame to the new file.
        size_t bytesWritten = fwrite(buffer, 1, bytesRead, output);

        // Close the output file and check for errors.
        if (bytesWritten != bytesRead) {
            printf("Failed to write to output file.\n");
        }
        fclose(output);
    }

    free(buffer);
    fclose(input);
}
```

This code simply reads a binary image file, allocates a buffer for each frame in the file and writes it into an image file. It does not include any specific JPEG encoding or resizing logic which would be highly dependent on your needs. Also, memory deallocation is handled only at the end of the function. 

To implement all of this, you will need a more detailed understanding of how to handle and process images in C, including image format handling, error handling, memory allocation/deallocation, file I/O, etc. It's also worth mentioning that performance optimization with different resolutions is another big part which can be complex to achieve depending on your requirements.
