["Here's a sample implementation of how you might start creating such a system using `pthread` for thread synchronization and memory allocation. Please note that this is just a skeleton implementation and doesn't cover all the details, but it can be a starting point:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int session_id;
    char *client_address;
    int server_resource_status; // 0 for inactive, 1 for active
} CONN_REQUEST_T;

CONN_REQUEST_T **connectionList = NULL;
int connectionCount = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void initConnectionSystem() {
    pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL);
    connectionList = malloc(sizeof(CONN_REQUEST_T*));
}

void handleConnectionRequest(int sessionId, char *clientAddress) {
    CONN_REQUEST_T *connection = malloc(sizeof(CONN_REQUEST_T));
    connection->session_id = sessionId;
    connection->client_address = strdup(clientAddress);
    connection->server_resource_status = 0; // initially inactive

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    ++connectionCount;
    connectionList = realloc(connectionList, sizeof(CONN_REQUEST_T*) * connectionCount);
    connectionList[connectionCount - 1] = connection;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void closeConnection(int sessionId) {
    int i;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (i = 0; i < connectionCount; ++i) {
        if (connectionList[i]->session_id == sessionId) {
            free(connectionList[i]->client_address);
            free(connectionList[i]);

            // Move the last connection to current position and decrease count
            --connectionCount;
            connectionList[i] = connectionList[connectionCount];
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void activateServerResource(int sessionId) {
    int i;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (i = 0; i < connectionCount; ++i) {
        if (connectionList[i]->session_id == sessionId) {
            connectionList[i]->server_resource_status = 1;
            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
``` 
This code uses a simple array of pointers to `CONN_REQUEST_T` structs, which allows for efficient memory allocation and deallocation. When a connection is closed or fails, the related memory is freed immediately.

The function `handleConnectionRequest()` is used to add new connections to the system. It takes a session ID and client address as arguments, creates a new `CONN_REQUEST_T` struct with this information, adds it to the list of connections, and ensures that no other thread can access the connection list while this operation is in progress by locking a mutex (pthread_mutex_lock).

The function `closeConnection()` removes a connection from the system when it's closed or fails. It searches for the connection with the given session ID, frees its memory, and then shifts all subsequent connections to fill the gap left by the removed one. This operation is also protected by a mutex lock.

The function `activateServerResource()` simulates server resource allocation when required, it changes the status of server resource associated with the given session ID from inactive (0) to active (1).

Please note that there are many other aspects such as connection pooling, timeout handling etc that need to be implemented based on specific requirements. This is just a basic example and doesn't cover all those points. 

Also please note this code may not work properly if multiple threads call `handleConnectionRequest()` or `closeConnection()` at the same time. If you want to handle this correctly, consider using more advanced thread synchronization methods like condition variables (pthread_cond).
"]

