['Here is a solution using Java's built-in library functions to perform file metadata extraction, exception handling, reflection for dynamic access of attributes and ObjectInputStream for deserialization from binary file. 

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.time.*;
import java.time.format.*;

public class FileMetadataExtractor {
    public static Map<String, List<File>> processFiles(List<Path> filePaths) throws IOException, ReflectiveOperationException {
        // Maps extensions to lists of files
        Map<String, List<File>> categorizedFiles = new HashMap<>();

        long totalProcessTime = 0;
        DateTimeFormatter dtf = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy/MM/dd HH:mm:ss");  

        for (Path filePath : filePaths) {
            File file = filePath.toFile();

            long startTime = System.nanoTime();
            String extension = "";

            try {
                if (file.exists() && file.isFile()) {
                    // Extract metadata
                    BasicFileAttributes attrs = Files.readAttributes(filePath, BasicFileAttributes.class);

                    System.out.println("Size: " + attrs.size());
                    System.out.println("Last Modified Time: " + dtf.format(attrs.lastModifiedTime().toInstant()));
                    // The method for retrieving permissions is different on Windows and Unix-based systems, here we assume Unix-style permissions
                    Set<PosixFilePermission> perms = Files.getPosixFilePermissions(filePath);
                    System.out.println("Permissions: " + perms);

                    extension = com.google.common.io.Files.getFileExtension(file.getName()); // Guava library method to get file extension
                } else {
                    throw new FileNotFoundException();
                }
            } catch (IOException | IllegalArgumentException e) {
                System.err.println("Error processing " + filePath + ": " + e.getMessage());
            } finally {
                long endTime = System.nanoTime();

                // Add to categorized files
                if (!extension.isEmpty()) {
                    categorizedFiles.computeIfAbsent(extension, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(file);
                }

                totalProcessTime += (endTime - startTime);
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Total Processing Time: " + totalProcessTime/1e6 + " ms");

        return categorizedFiles;
    }

    public static Object deserializeFromFile(String filePath) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            return in.readObject();
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.err.println("Error deserializing from " + filePath + ": " + e.getMessage());
            throw e;
        }
    }
}
```
For the `deserializeFromFile` method, you will need to have the appropriate library (like gson or jackson) in your classpath to handle deserialization from binary files. Please note that this is a simple example and may not cover all cases but it should serve as a good starting point for more complicated scenarios. 

Please replace `com.google.common.io.Files.getFileExtension(file.getName());` with your own method of extracting file extensions if you're not using Guava library.

Also remember, to use ObjectInputStream to deserialize object from the binary file make sure that the class for the object must implement `Serializable` interface. And also note that `ObjectInputStream` and `FileInputStream` can throw IOException so it is good practice to handle them properly.
']

