public class NetworkUtil {

    private static final String IP_REGEX = "^([0-9]{1,3})\\.([0-9]{1,3})\\.([0-9]{1,3})\\.([0-9]{1,3})$"; 
    private static Pattern pattern;

    static { // Initialized during class loading
        try{
            pattern = Pattern.compile(IP_REGEX);
        }catch(PatternSyntaxException e){
           System.out.println("Error in regular expression: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    } 

    private NetworkUtil() { // Private constructor to prevent instantiation of utility class
    }

    public static synchronized boolean validateIPAddress(String ip) { 
        try{
            Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(ip); 
            return matcher.matches();
        }catch(RuntimeException ex){ //Handle exceptions gracefully
           System.out.println("Error during IP validation: " + ex.getMessage());
           return false;
        }
    } 
}
```
This code assumes that the `parseHeader` function is available and properly handles packet data. It also uses Java's built-in regex library to validate IP addresses, ensuring thread safety with the synchronized keyword for multithreaded calls. This solution also includes error handling in both C and Java.
