However, I can guide you on how to start building your own C++ microservice and connect it with Redis cache system which will help you in creating a scalable cloud-native service.

1. Create a simple web server: You could use the Boost ASIO library or any other C++ HTTP library for this purpose. Here is an example of how to create a simple HTTP server using Boost.Asio:

```cpp
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <iostream>

using namespace boost::asio;
using ip::tcp;
using std::string;
using std::cout;
using std::endl;

string read_(tcp::socket& socket) {
    boost::asio::streambuf buf;
    boost::asio::read_until(socket, buf, "\n");
    string data = boost::asio::buffer_cast<const char*>(buf.data());
    return data;
}
void send_(tcp::socket& socket, const string& message) {
    const string msg = message + "\n";
    boost::asio::write(socket, boost::asio::buffer(message));
}

int main() {
    boost::asio::io_service io_service;
    //listen for new connection
    tcp::acceptor acceptor_(io_service, tcp::endpoint(tcp::v4(), 1234));
    //socket creation 
    tcp::socket socket_(io_service);
    //waiting for connection
    acceptor_.accept(socket_);
    //read operation
    string message = read_(socket_);
    cout << message << endl;
    //write operation
    send_(socket_, "Hello From Server!");
    return 0;
}
```
2. Integrate with Redis: You can use the hiredis library for C++ to connect and interact with a Redis server. Here is an example of how you could set and get values from a Redis cache in your application:

```cpp
#include <hiredis/hiredis.h>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    redisContext *c = redisConnect("127.0.0.1", 6379);
    if (c != NULL && c->err) {
        printf("Error: %s\n", c->errstr);
        // handle error
    } else {
        const char *key = "foo";
        redisReply *reply;

        reply = (redisReply*) redisCommand(c, "SET %b %s %b", key, strlen(key), "bar", strlen("bar"));
        freeReplyObject(reply);

        reply = (redisReply*) redisCommand(c, "GET %b", key, strlen(key));
        printf("%s\n", reply->str);
        freeReplyObject(reply);
    }
    // disconnect
    redisFree(c);
}
```
Please note that this is a very basic example and real world applications would require a lot more complexity. It's also important to remember that C++ is not the best language for writing cloud-native services, as it doesn't have great support for concurrency and asynchronous programming compared to other languages like Go or NodeJS. For such tasks, you might want to consider using a different language like Go or Rust which are designed for these kinds of tasks.
