However, for your second requirement which is to write a Java method to compute the MD5 hash of a large file, here's an example that meets those requirements. However, please note that you'll need to import necessary libraries and handle exceptions as necessary:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, IOException {
        System.out.println("MD5 hash: " + getFileChecksum(new File("/path/to/your/file")));
    }

    private static String getFileChecksum(File file) throws IOException, NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");

        try (InputStream inputStream = new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(file))) {
            byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
            int bytesRead;
            while ((bytesRead = inputStream.read(buffer)) != -1) {
                digest.update(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
            }
        }

        // Convert the hash value to a hexadecimal string
        byte[] hashBytes = digest.digest();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : hashBytes) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
This program computes the MD5 checksum of a file and returns it as a hexadecimal string. It uses buffering to minimize disk I/O operations, which should improve performance on large files. This Java method can be integrated into a larger system with additional features such as concurrency control, error handling, progress indicators, and cluster-distributed processing.
