void* hashFile(char *filePath, unsigned char *result) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filePath, "rb");
    if (!file) return NULL;

    MD5_CTX context;
    int bytes;
    unsigned char data[1024]; // Assuming 1kB chunks.

    MD5_Init(&context);

    long totalBytes = ftell(file);
    rewind(file);
    double processed = 0;
    while ((bytes = fread(data, 1, 1024, file)) != 0) {
        MD5_Update(&context, data, bytes);
        processed += bytes;

        // This is where you'd implement your progress indicator. Replace with a call to your own function or print statement.
        printf("Hashing Progress: %.2f%%\n", (processed / totalBytes) * 100.0);
    }

    MD5_Final(result, &context);
    fclose(file);

    return result;
}
```
This function `hashFile()` takes a file path and an unsigned char array for the results of the hash computation. It reads the file in chunks, updating the hash context with each chunk read from the file. The MD5_Final call is used to finish the MD5 computation and store the result into `result`. 

Note that this assumes that you are using OpenSSL's MD5 library. You would need to include it in your project for compilation. Also, if dealing with larger files, you might want to increase the chunk size from 1024 bytes or consider reading the file in a separate thread for better performance. This code doesn't take into account error handling which is also important when writing production-level software but it's out of scope here due to space constraints.
